    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p><strong>Bone density rises mainly through heavy, weight-bearing exercise, with nutrition acting as the permission slip rather than the stimulus.</strong> In the LIFTMOR trial, twice-weekly heavy resistance and impact training raised lumbar spine density by 2.9% in eight months while the control group lost 1.2%.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Load is the lever:</strong> heavy resistance plus impact work, roughly 30 minutes twice a week, beat low-intensity home exercise in an eight-month randomised trial.</li>
        <li><strong>Calcium target:</strong> 1,000mg a day for Indian adults under ICMR-NIN 2020 — about four glasses of milk, which most Indian diets do not reach.</li>
        <li><strong>Vitamin D decides absorption:</strong> in 2.2 million Indian test results, 46.5% were deficient and another 26% insufficient.</li>
        <li><strong>The honest limit:</strong> calcium and vitamin D pills alone did not cut fractures in community-dwelling adults, which is why no supplement replaces the training.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

