      <h2 id="can-it-rise">Can you actually increase bone density, or only slow the loss?</h2>

      <p>
        You can genuinely increase it, but the gains are measured in single-digit percentages over
        months, not weeks. Bone is living tissue that is broken down and rebuilt continuously, so
        the density on your DEXA report is the running balance between those two processes. Shift
        the balance and the number moves.
      </p>

      <p>
        Most people reach peak bone mass somewhere between the ages of 25 and 30, after which the
        balance tips slowly the other way. For women the drop accelerates sharply around menopause,
        which is why the <a href="/pages/collagen-for-women">decade-by-decade picture</a> matters
        more than a single reading. The practical consequence: the earlier you load your skeleton,
        the more you have to spend later.
      </p>

      <p>
        In India this is not a niche concern. The International Osteoporosis Foundation's 2025
        country profile notes there is no centralised fracture database here, so national figures
        are estimates rather than counts — and the commonly quoted estimates run to tens of
        millions of people with osteoporosis or low bone mass. Treat the headline numbers with
        caution; treat your own DEXA score as the real data.
      </p>

      <h2 id="exercise">Which exercise increases bone density?</h2>

      <p>
        Heavy resistance training combined with impact, done consistently. This is the only
        intervention with randomised evidence of raising bone density by a meaningful margin in
        under a year, and nothing in a supplement aisle substitutes for it.
      </p>

      <p>
        The clearest trial is LIFTMOR, published in the <em>Journal of Bone and Mineral Research</em>
        in 2018. Researchers randomised 101 postmenopausal women with low bone mass (a T-score below
        −1.0) to either eight months of supervised high-intensity resistance and impact training —
        twice weekly, 30 minutes, five sets of five repetitions above 85% of one-rep max — or a
        home-based low-intensity programme. The results split cleanly in two directions.
      </p>

