      <p>
        Two things are worth noticing. The training group gained at the spine, which is the usual
        story. But the more useful finding is the control group's steady loss at both sites: doing
        gentle exercise was not a holding pattern. The gap between the groups is larger than either
        number alone.
      </p>

      <p>
        The trial was small, participants were not blinded, and it was supervised by trained staff
        — which is exactly how heavy lifting should be introduced to someone with low bone mass.
        Do not read "five sets of five above 85% of one-rep max" as a licence to load a barbell
        unsupervised with a spine you have not had scanned.
      </p>

      <p>
        What does not work as well is the exercise most people pick. Swimming and cycling put very
        little force through the skeleton, and reviews of athletes in non-weight-bearing sports have
        repeatedly found lower bone density than in runners and ball-sport players, sometimes lower
        than inactive peers. They are excellent for your heart. They are a weak signal to bone.
        Adding jump training to swimmers and cyclists improved density in one randomised trial,
        which tells you the missing ingredient was impact, not effort.
      </p>

      <h2 id="calcium-d">How much calcium and vitamin D do you need?</h2>

      <p>
        For Indian adults the calcium target is 1,000mg a day under the ICMR-NIN 2020
        recommendations, and vitamin D needs to be sufficient for any of it to be absorbed. Food
        first is now the formal position: the Endocrine Society of India's Skeletal Health
        Taskforce published its first calcium consensus in the <em>Indian Journal of Endocrinology
        and Metabolism</em>, advising clinicians to estimate what a patient already eats before
        prescribing anything, and to aim for 800–1,000mg a day from food and supplements combined
        where intake falls short.
      </p>

      <p>
        The arithmetic is what makes this hard in practice. A glass of milk carries roughly 250mg of
        calcium, so hitting 1,000mg from dairy alone means about four servings a day. Indian
        vegetarian diets built on rice or wheat with little dairy can fall well short unless ragi,
        sesame, amaranth, curd, paneer and leafy greens are doing real work. Needs rise further in
        pregnancy, during breastfeeding and after menopause.
      </p>

      <p>
        Vitamin D is the bigger Indian problem, and sunshine does not solve it. An analysis of more
        than 2.2 million test results from 2019 to January 2025 found 46.5% of people tested were
        deficient and a further 26% insufficient, with South India highest at 51.6% and teenagers
        worst affected at 66.9%. Those are people who chose to get tested, so the figure overstates
        the general population — but not by enough to ignore.
      </p>

