      <p>
        Now the inconvenient part, because it changes how you should think about pills. The US
        Preventive Services Task Force recommends <em>against</em> daily supplementation with 400 IU
        or less of vitamin D3 plus 1,000mg or less of calcium for preventing fractures in
        community-dwelling postmenopausal women, and found the evidence insufficient at higher
        doses. A meta-analysis of older adults found combined supplementation reduced fracture risk
        overall, but in community-dwelling adults specifically the estimate was a relative risk of
        0.89 with a confidence interval of 0.76 to 1.04 — which includes no effect at all.
      </p>

      <p>
        That evidence does not say calcium and vitamin D are pointless. It says that <em>correcting
        a shortfall</em> is the goal, and that topping up someone who is already replete does not
        buy them stronger bones. The benefit shows up in deficiency, in institutionalised older
        adults, and as the nutritional floor underneath training — not as a standalone treatment.
        It is also why the Endocrine Society of India advises against routinely exceeding 2,000mg of
        calcium a day, citing the risk of vascular calcification. Knowing whether you have a
        shortfall in the first place is a blood test rather than a symptom check, because the
        <a href="/pages/vitamin-d-deficiency-symptoms">symptoms of vitamin D deficiency</a> are
        mostly non-specific and often absent altogether.
      </p>

