      <h2 id="collagen-protein">Does protein or collagen help bone density?</h2>

      <p>
        Adequate protein helps modestly, and collagen peptides have one genuinely interesting
        12-month trial behind them — but both sit below exercise in the order of importance, and the
        collagen evidence is thinner than the marketing suggests.
      </p>

      <p>
        On protein: a 2019 systematic review in adults over 65 found higher intakes tracked with
        higher femoral neck and total hip density, and pooled four cohort studies to a hazard ratio
        of 0.89 for hip fracture. But a 2009 meta-analysis of randomised trials found protein
        supplementation improved lumbar spine density with no association with hip fracture risk,
        and a broader 2017 review across the lifespan found no association with osteoporotic
        fractures overall, while confirming small benefits and no harm. Reassuring on the old
        "protein leaches calcium" worry. Not a fracture prevention strategy. If you are unsure
        whether you are hitting your protein target on an Indian vegetarian diet, that
        <a href="/pages/boost-collagen-naturally">arithmetic is worth doing</a>.
      </p>

      <p>
        On collagen: König and colleagues, publishing in <em>Nutrients</em> in 2018, gave 131
        postmenopausal women with reduced bone density (DXA T-score of −1 or lower at the femoral
        neck or lumbar spine) either 5g of specific collagen peptides or 5g of maltodextrin daily
        for 12 months. Density rose significantly at both the spine and the femoral neck in the
        collagen group compared with placebo, alongside a rise in the bone formation marker P1NP.
        That is a real result with a real duration, and it is consistent with what collagen peptides
        appear to do in <a href="/pages/collagen-for-joint-pain">cartilage and joint trials</a>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Three caveats belong next to it. The trial used one specific peptide preparation, and was
        funded by the company that makes it. It has not been widely replicated at 12 months. And a
        significant percentage change in density is not the same as a demonstrated reduction in
        fractures, which is what actually matters to a patient. One good trial is a reason for
        interest, not a reason for certainty.
      </p>

