      <h2 id="plan">A 12-month plan that moves the number</h2>

      <p>
        Bone remodels slowly, so a plan shorter than a year is a plan to measure noise. In order of
        how much each step is likely to matter:
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li><strong>Get a baseline.</strong> A DEXA scan gives you the T-score the rest of this depends on. Without it you are guessing, and the right level of aggression differs completely between normal density, osteopenia and osteoporosis. <a href="/pages/osteopenia-vs-osteoporosis">Reading your DEXA report</a> explains where the T-score bands sit and why most fragility fractures happen in people labelled osteopenic rather than osteoporotic.</li>
        <li><strong>Test vitamin D, and correct it properly.</strong> Given the Indian deficiency rates, assume nothing. A tested deficiency is corrected on a doctor's dosing schedule, not with a maintenance supplement.</li>
        <li><strong>Count your calcium for three days.</strong> Add up what you actually eat against the 1,000mg target. Close the gap with food where you can — curd, paneer, milk, ragi, sesame, amaranth, leafy greens.</li>
        <li><strong>Start supervised resistance training.</strong> Twice weekly is the trial dose. Progressive load with a physiotherapist or a coach who has seen your scan, and impact work only if your scan allows it.</li>
        <li><strong>Hit your protein target daily.</strong> Adequate, not heroic. This is the floor the training builds on, and most Indian vegetarian diets sit below it.</li>
        <li><strong>Remove the subtractions.</strong> Smoking, heavy alcohol and being underweight all work against bone, and no supplement outruns them.</li>
        <li><strong>Rescan at 12 months.</strong> Earlier scans mostly measure machine variation rather than your skeleton.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        If joint pain is what is stopping you from loading your legs at all, that is a separate
        problem to solve first — the
        <a href="/pages/supplements-for-knee-pain">evidence map for knee pain</a> is the better
        starting point there.
      </p>

