      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="jr-faq">
        <details>
          <summary>How long does it take to increase bone density?</summary>
          <p>Expect to measure change in months, not weeks. The LIFTMOR training trial ran for eight months and the collagen peptide trial for 12 months before significant density changes were reported. Most clinicians rescan at 12 months, because a scan taken earlier is likely to show machine variation rather than a real shift in your skeleton.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can you increase bone density after 60?</summary>
          <p>Yes. The strongest training evidence comes from postmenopausal women with already low bone mass, averaging well past 60 in the collagen trial, where participants were aged 46 to 80. Older bone responds to load; the difference is that supervision matters more, and impact work needs to be cleared against your scan first.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is walking enough to increase bone density?</summary>
          <p>Walking helps maintain bone and is far better than nothing, but on its own it is a weak stimulus for raising density. In the LIFTMOR trial the low-intensity comparison group lost density at both the spine and the femoral neck over eight months. Progressive resistance training, and impact work where it is safe, is what produced gains.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How much calcium do Indian adults need for bone density?</summary>
          <p>The ICMR-NIN 2020 recommendation for adults is 1,000mg a day, and the Endocrine Society of India's calcium consensus advises aiming for 800 to 1,000mg daily from food and supplements combined where intake falls short. A glass of milk supplies roughly 250mg, so food should do most of the work before a supplement is added.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does collagen increase bone density?</summary>
          <p>One 12-month randomised trial supports it. König and colleagues gave 131 postmenopausal women with reduced bone density 5g of specific collagen peptides or placebo daily, and density rose significantly at the spine and femoral neck in the collagen group. The trial used one specific peptide and was industry-funded, so treat it as promising rather than settled.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Why is vitamin D deficiency so common in India despite the sunshine?</summary>
          <p>Across 2.2 million Indian test results, 46.5% were vitamin D deficient and 26% insufficient. Indoor work, clothing that covers most of the skin, higher melanin, air pollution and avoiding midday sun all reduce how much the skin actually makes. A blood test is the only way to know your level, and correcting a deficiency needs a doctor's dosing schedule.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Should I take a calcium supplement for bone density?</summary>
          <p>Only if your diet falls short or your doctor identifies a higher need, such as pregnancy, breastfeeding, menopause or diagnosed osteoporosis. The US Preventive Services Task Force recommends against low-dose calcium and vitamin D for fracture prevention in community-dwelling postmenopausal women, and the Endocrine Society of India advises against exceeding 2,000mg of calcium daily.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Do swimming and cycling build bone?</summary>
          <p>Not effectively. Both put very little force through the skeleton, and reviews of athletes in non-weight-bearing sports have found lower bone density than in runners and ball-sport players. One randomised trial found that adding jump training to swimmers and cyclists did improve density, which suggests the missing element is impact rather than effort.</p>
        </details>
      </div>

